== Inferences

1\. From this we may learn how great the error is of those who are for discarding all religious affections, as having nothing solid or substantial in them.

There seems to be too much of a disposition this way prevailing in this land at this time. Many who in the recent extraordinary season appeared to have great religious affections did not show a right temper of mind, and ran into many errors in the time of their affection and the heat of their zeal. The high affections of many seem to have come to nothing so soon, and some who seemed for a while to be mightily lifted up and swallowed up with joy and zeal seem to have returned like the dog to his vomit. And because of this, religious affections in general have lost their credit with great numbers, as though true religion did not consist in them at all. So easily and naturally do we run from one extreme to another. A little while ago we were in the other extreme. There was a prevailing disposition to look on all high religious affections as eminent exercises of true grace, without much inquiry into the nature and source of those affections and the way in which they arose. If persons only appeared to be very much moved and lifted up, so as to be full of religious talk and to express themselves with great warmth and earnestness, and to be #emph[filled,] or #emph[very full,] as the phrases were, it was too much the custom to conclude, without further examination, that such persons were full of the Spirit of God and had eminent experience of his gracious influences. This was the extreme that prevailed three or four years ago. But lately, instead of #emph[esteeming] and #emph[wondering at] all religious #emph[affections without distinction,] it is much more common to #emph[reject] and #emph[discard] all #emph[without distinction.] In this the craftiness of #emph[Satan] appears. While he saw that #emph[affections] were much in fashion, knowing that the greater part of the land were not versed in such things, and had not had much experience of great #emph[religious affections] to enable them to judge well of them and distinguish between true and false, he knew he could best play his game by sowing tares among the wheat and mingling #emph[false affections] with the works of God’s Spirit. He knew this was a likely way to deceive and eternally ruin many souls, to wound religion greatly in the saints and entangle them in a dreadful wilderness, and in time to bring all religion into disrepute. But now, when the bad consequences of these #emph[false affections] appear, and it has become very plain that some of those emotions which made a dazzling show, and were greatly admired by many, were in reality nothing, the devil sees that it is in his interest to go to work another way. He sees it is in his interest to do his utmost to spread and establish a persuasion that all affections and felt emotions of the mind in things of religion are not to be regarded at all, but are rather to be avoided and carefully guarded against as things of a harmful tendency. This, he knows, is the way to bring all religion down to a mere lifeless formality, to shut out effectually the power of godliness and everything that is spiritual, and to have all true Christianity turned out of doors. For although true religion must indeed have something else besides affection, yet true religion consists so much in the affections that there can be no true religion without them. He who has no religious affection is in a state of spiritual death, and is wholly without the powerful, life-giving, saving influences of the Spirit of God on his heart. As there is no true religion where there is nothing but affection, so there is no true religion where there is no #emph[religious affection.] On the one hand, there must be light in the understanding as well as an #emph[affected,] fervent heart; where there is heat without light, there can be nothing divine or heavenly in that heart. So on the other hand, where there is a kind of light without heat, a head stored with notions and theories, with a cold and unaffected heart, there can be nothing divine in that light; that knowledge is no true spiritual knowledge of divine things. If the great things of religion are rightly understood, they will affect the heart. The reason men are not affected by such infinitely great, important, glorious and wonderful things as they often hear and read of in the word of God is without doubt that they are blind. If they were not, it would be impossible, and utterly inconsistent with human nature, for their hearts to be anything other than strongly impressed and greatly moved by such things.

This way of slighting all religious #emph[affections] is the way to harden the hearts of men exceedingly, to encourage them in their dullness and insensibility, to keep them in a state of spiritual death as long as they live, and to bring them at last to eternal death. The prejudice against #emph[religious affections] that prevails in the land at this day evidently has a fearful effect. It hardens the hearts of sinners, damps the graces of many of the saints, deadens the life and power of religion, cuts off the effect of the ordinances, and holds us down in a state of dullness and apathy. And it no doubt causes many persons to offend God greatly by entertaining poor and low thoughts of the extraordinary work he has recently done in this land.

And for persons to despise and cry down all religious #emph[affections] is the way to shut all religion out of their own hearts and to make thorough work of ruining their souls.

Those who condemn high affections in others are certainly not likely to have high affections themselves. And let it be considered that those who have only a little religious affection certainly have only a little religion. And those who condemn others for their #emph[religious affections,] and have none themselves, have no religion.

There are false #emph[affections,] and there are true. A man’s having #emph[much affection] does not prove that he has any true religion. But if he has #emph[no affection,] it proves that he has no true religion. The right way is not to reject all affections, nor to approve all, but to distinguish between affections, approving some and rejecting others: separating the wheat from the chaff, the gold from the dross, the precious from the vile.

2\. If it is true that true religion lies much in the #emph[affections,] we may infer from this that such means are to be desired as have much tendency to move the affections. Such books, such a way of preaching the word and administering the ordinances, and such a way of worshiping God in prayer and singing praises, are much to be desired as tend to affect deeply the hearts of those who attend these means.

Such means would formerly have been highly approved and applauded by most of the people of the land, as the most excellent and profitable, and as having the greatest tendency to promote the ends of the means of grace. But the prevailing taste seems lately to have changed strangely. The moving manner of praying and preaching that would formerly have been admired and praised, and for this very reason, that it tended so much to move the affections, now in great multitudes immediately stirs up disgust, and moves no other affections than those of displeasure and contempt.

Perhaps formerly most people (at least of the common people) were in the extreme of looking too much for an emotional manner in public worship. But now a very great part of the people seem to have gone far into the contrary extreme. Indeed, there may be such means as have a great tendency to stir up the passions of weak and ignorant persons, and yet have no great tendency to benefit their souls. For though they may tend to stir up affections, they may have little or no tendency to stir up gracious affections, or any affections tending to grace. But without doubt, if the things of religion are treated in the means used according to their nature, and set forth truly, in a way that tends to convey correct perceptions and a right judgment of them, the more they tend to move the affections, the better.

3\. If true religion lies much in the affections, we may learn from this what great cause we have to be ashamed and confounded before God that we are not more affected by the great things of religion. It appears from what has been said that this comes from our having so little true religion.

God has given mankind affections for the same purpose for which he has given all the faculties and principles of the human soul, namely, that they might serve man’s chief end and the great business for which God created him, that is, the business of religion. And yet how common it is among mankind that their affections are much more exercised and engaged in other matters than in religion! In things that concern men’s worldly interest, their outward delights, their honor and reputation, and their natural relations, their desires are eager, their appetites vehement, their love warm and affectionate, their zeal ardent. In these things their hearts are tender and feeling, easily moved, deeply impressed, much concerned, very deeply affected and greatly engaged; much cast down with grief at worldly losses, and highly lifted up with joy at worldly success and prosperity. But how unfeeling and unmoved most men are about the great things of another world! How dull their affections are! How heavy and hard their hearts are in these matters! Here their love is cold, their desires feeble, their zeal low and their gratitude small. They can sit and hear of the infinite height and depth and length and breadth of the love of God in Christ Jesus: of his giving his infinitely dear Son to be offered up as a sacrifice for the sins of men; and of the unparalleled love of the innocent, holy and tender Lamb of God, shown in his dying agonies, his bloody sweat, his loud and bitter cries and his bleeding heart, and all this for enemies, to redeem them from deserved, eternal burnings and to bring them to unspeakable and everlasting joy and glory. And yet they can be cold and heavy, unfeeling and indifferent! Where is the proper exercise of our affections, if not here? What requires them more? And what can be a fit occasion for their lively and vigorous exercise, if not such an occasion as this? Can anything be set before us that is greater and more important? Anything more wonderful and astonishing? Or anything that concerns our interest more nearly? Can we suppose that the wise Creator planted such principles as the affections in human nature to be of use to us and to be exercised on certain proper occasions, but to lie still on such an occasion as this? Can any Christian who believes the truth of these things entertain such thoughts?

If we ought ever to exercise our affections at all, and if the Creator has not made human nature unwisely, in making these principles a part of it when they are vain and useless, then they ought to be exercised about the objects that are most worthy of them. But is there anything Christians can find in heaven or earth so worthy to be the object of their wonder and love, their earnest and longing desires, their hope, their rejoicing and their fervent zeal, as the things held out to us in the gospel of Jesus Christ? In the gospel, not only are the things declared that are most worthy to affect us, but they are set forth in the most affecting manner. The glory and beauty of the blessed Jehovah, which is in itself most worthy to be the object of our wonder and love, is set forth there in the most affecting manner that can be conceived, as it appears shining in all its luster in the face of an incarnate, infinitely loving, meek, compassionate, dying Redeemer. All the virtues of the Lamb of God, his humility, patience, meekness, submission, obedience, love and compassion, are set before us in a manner more fitted to move our affections than any that can be imagined. For they all had their greatest trial and their highest exercise, and so their brightest display, when he was in the most affecting circumstances: when he was under his last sufferings, those unutterable and unparalleled sufferings he endured from his tender love and pity to us. There too the hateful nature of our sins is shown in the most affecting manner possible, as we see their dreadful effects in what our Redeemer, who undertook to answer for us, suffered for them. And there we have the most affecting displays of God’s hatred of sin, and his wrath and justice in punishing it. We see his justice in its strictness and inflexibility, and his wrath in its terribleness, in his so dreadfully punishing our sins in one who was infinitely dear to him and loving to us. God has so ordered things in the work of our redemption, and in his glorious dealings revealed to us in the gospel, as though everything were purposely arranged to have the greatest possible tendency to reach our hearts in their most tender part, and to move our affections most deeply and strongly. How great cause, then, we have to be humbled to the dust that we are not more affected!
